WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:02.190
- [Narrator] Four decades
after its creation,

00:00:02.190 --> 00:00:06.600
Silicon Valley Bank was the
16th largest bank in the US.

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:09.990
It took just a day and a
half for it to fall apart.

00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:11.760
- This is the second biggest bank failure

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:14.400
that's ever happened in the United States.

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:16.950
- [Narrator] Once seen as a
major tech banking player,

00:00:16.950 --> 00:00:20.205
SVB's stunning collapse
spurred other bank closures,

00:00:20.205 --> 00:00:22.200
rattled global markets

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:24.450
and threatened the livelihoods of startups

00:00:24.450 --> 00:00:26.070
across the country.

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:28.113
So, what went wrong?

00:00:29.670 --> 00:00:32.370
Silicon Valley Bank opened in 1983

00:00:32.370 --> 00:00:34.590
to serve fledgling tech companies.

00:00:34.590 --> 00:00:36.242
Eventually, nearly half of the country's

00:00:36.242 --> 00:00:38.123
venture capital backed technology

00:00:38.123 --> 00:00:42.060
and life sciences companies
would rely on SVB.

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:46.560
Roku, ROBLOX and many others
put millions into the bank,

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:49.980
helping SVB become one
of the nation's largest.

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:50.820
- Some of these clients

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:52.860
would definitely be considered risky.

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:54.570
These are companies that move quickly

00:00:54.570 --> 00:00:55.980
and their money moves quickly.

00:00:55.980 --> 00:00:57.300
When things sort of went off the rails,

00:00:57.300 --> 00:00:59.280
they were quick to move
their money out of the bank.

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:00.510
- [Narrator] After the banking crisis

00:01:00.510 --> 00:01:02.100
that triggered the Great Recession,

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:05.310
President Barack Obama
signed the Dodd Frank Act,

00:01:05.310 --> 00:01:09.270
making banks like SVB
face stricter regulation.

00:01:09.270 --> 00:01:10.620
- It is designed to make sure

00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:12.990
that everybody follows
the same set of rules.

00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:13.823
- [Narrator] But eight years later

00:01:13.823 --> 00:01:15.630
during the Trump administration,

00:01:15.630 --> 00:01:20.010
some of those regulations on
smaller banks were rolled back.

00:01:20.010 --> 00:01:23.250
- By liberating small banks
from excessive bureaucracy

00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:25.464
and that's what it was, bureaucracy,

00:01:25.464 --> 00:01:30.330
we are unleashing the economic
potential of our people.

00:01:30.330 --> 00:01:32.790
- Some of the rules were
rolled back for these banks

00:01:32.790 --> 00:01:35.640
that had less than $250 billion in assets.

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:37.260
So you have the biggest banks

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:39.180
like Bank of America and Chase.

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:40.410
Those have very strict rules

00:01:40.410 --> 00:01:43.740
but the ones that are a step
below have looser rules.

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:46.920
- [Narrator] Two years later,
SVB was flooded with cash

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:49.950
as businesses deposited
more during the pandemic.

00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:54.630
Deposits tripled in two
years to $189 billion,

00:01:54.630 --> 00:01:59.040
making 2021 SVBs most
profitable year ever.

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:00.330
- [Becker] Our core business

00:02:00.330 --> 00:02:02.220
continues to fire on all cylinders.

00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:04.830
- [Narrator] So SVB
Financial took that cash

00:02:04.830 --> 00:02:06.941
and bought tens of billions of dollars

00:02:06.941 --> 00:02:09.090
of longer term US treasuries

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:11.940
and government backed mortgage securities.

00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:14.340
Products usually considered safe.

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:16.770
- People and companies just put
money in their bank accounts

00:02:16.770 --> 00:02:18.690
and banks said, we have
to do something with this.

00:02:18.690 --> 00:02:19.980
We have to earn some income on it.

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:21.301
So they bought bonds with it.

00:02:21.301 --> 00:02:23.880
- [Narrator] SVB's securities portfolio

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:27.350
rose about a hundred billion
dollars in under a year.

00:02:27.350 --> 00:02:29.747
- After SVB had stockpiled

00:02:29.747 --> 00:02:32.361
that hundred billion
dollars plus in bonds,

00:02:32.361 --> 00:02:34.770
all of a sudden interest rates rose.

00:02:34.770 --> 00:02:36.030
- Committee anticipates that

00:02:36.030 --> 00:02:38.040
ongoing increases in the target range

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:40.620
for the federal funds
rate will be appropriate.

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:42.630
- What happens when
interest rates rise is,

00:02:42.630 --> 00:02:44.130
bond prices fall.

00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:47.700
Banks, including SVB that were
holding onto a lot of bonds,

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:49.260
were sitting on a bunch of losses.

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:51.180
- [Narrator] Soon, SVB's investments

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:55.470
were worth $17 billion
less than their fair value.

00:02:55.470 --> 00:02:56.387
- And so all of a sudden,

00:02:56.387 --> 00:03:00.000
the gap between what SVB
had paid for those bonds

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:01.470
and what they were worth on paper

00:03:01.470 --> 00:03:03.660
had jumped to more than $17 billion,

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:04.648
and that was the key risk

00:03:04.648 --> 00:03:08.130
that would eventually
lead to SVB's undoing.

00:03:08.130 --> 00:03:09.510
- [Narrator] Making matters worse,

00:03:09.510 --> 00:03:13.140
as interest rates rose,
new deposits shrank,

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:17.070
falling nearly $30 billion
from March to December.

00:03:17.070 --> 00:03:19.320
Talking about the end of 2022,

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:21.870
CEO Greg Becker told CNBC,

00:03:21.870 --> 00:03:24.270
- We kind of felt that that bottoming out.

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:25.530
We've kind of felt that we were

00:03:25.530 --> 00:03:27.510
kind of at that, that lower point.

00:03:27.510 --> 00:03:29.640
- [Narrator] The vast majority
of the bank's deposits

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:32.760
were held in just 37,000 accounts

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:35.490
that held more than $250,000.

00:03:35.490 --> 00:03:38.010
The amount insured by the FDIC.

00:03:38.010 --> 00:03:40.950
Then in a regulatory filing on March 8th,

00:03:40.950 --> 00:03:43.954
SVB announced it sold a
large chunk of securities

00:03:43.954 --> 00:03:46.751
at a loss of about $1.8 billion

00:03:46.751 --> 00:03:49.920
to help it cover that decline in deposits.

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:52.110
- The regulatory filing that Wednesday

00:03:52.110 --> 00:03:53.610
sparked a lot of fear.

00:03:53.610 --> 00:03:55.560
The stock fell a tremendous amount,

00:03:55.560 --> 00:03:57.090
which is never a good sign.

00:03:57.090 --> 00:03:59.497
- [Narrator] And investors
were already on edge.

00:03:59.497 --> 00:04:01.650
Crypto focused bank Silvergate

00:04:01.650 --> 00:04:03.807
had just announced it would wind down

00:04:03.807 --> 00:04:06.480
and return all deposits.

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:09.180
So the bottom was about to fall out.

00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:11.525
Startup CEOs began receiving urgent calls

00:04:11.525 --> 00:04:15.090
from panicked venture capital investors.

00:04:15.090 --> 00:04:18.330
- He was out of breath like
he had just run a marathon

00:04:18.330 --> 00:04:21.240
and he said, take your money out of SVB.

00:04:21.240 --> 00:04:22.380
Go into your account,

00:04:22.380 --> 00:04:24.840
take your money out as soon as possible.

00:04:24.840 --> 00:04:26.940
- [Narrator] What started
as a trickle of withdrawals

00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:28.980
quickly turned into a tidal wave

00:04:28.980 --> 00:04:30.870
as word spread across the valley.

00:04:30.870 --> 00:04:34.140
More and more startups pulled their cash.

00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:36.300
It was a run on the bank

00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:38.940
and the beginning of the end for SVB.

00:04:38.940 --> 00:04:42.510
The next day, the bank's stock
price went into free fall

00:04:42.510 --> 00:04:46.800
as customers tried to withdraw
$42 billion in deposits.

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:49.184
SVB ran out of cash.

00:04:49.184 --> 00:04:52.230
- All of a sudden, everyone's
saying, whoa, wait a minute.

00:04:52.230 --> 00:04:53.670
This bank is risky.

00:04:53.670 --> 00:04:55.470
The bank has enough
money to cover deposits

00:04:55.470 --> 00:04:58.740
if they come out in sort of
a peaceful, orderly fashion.

00:04:58.740 --> 00:05:02.400
When everyone's racing for
the exits at once, it doesn't.

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:05.100
- [Narrator] That day,
regulators seized the bank.

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:08.220
The FDIC said in a statement
that customers would have

00:05:08.220 --> 00:05:11.807
full access to their insured
deposits in three days.

00:05:11.807 --> 00:05:16.680
But the bank had more than
$151 billion worth of deposits

00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:19.980
at the end of 2022 that weren't insured.

00:05:19.980 --> 00:05:23.340
They were over the $250,000 limit.

00:05:23.340 --> 00:05:25.141
Two days after SVB's collapse

00:05:25.141 --> 00:05:28.260
a second bank with a
different set of problems,

00:05:28.260 --> 00:05:32.100
Signature failed and was
seized by regulators.

00:05:32.100 --> 00:05:34.890
The third largest failure in history.

00:05:34.890 --> 00:05:36.480
- And now people are
starting to worry about

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:37.890
other banks as well.

00:05:37.890 --> 00:05:42.120
It's very much kind of a a
whack-a-mole situation right now.

00:05:42.120 --> 00:05:43.093
Federal officials, state officials,

00:05:43.093 --> 00:05:45.660
everyone's in sort of
this unenviable position

00:05:45.660 --> 00:05:48.540
of trying to shore up confidence
in the banking industry

00:05:48.540 --> 00:05:51.120
so it doesn't turn into
an even bigger panic.

00:05:51.120 --> 00:05:52.800
- [Narrator] On Sunday, March 12th,

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:54.120
regulators announced that

00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:58.568
even uninsured deposits over
$250,000 from the two banks

00:05:58.568 --> 00:06:00.120
would be covered.

00:06:00.120 --> 00:06:03.300
on Monday, March 14th,
Signature told clients

00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:06.935
that due to the US Treasury
Federal Reserve and FDIC,

00:06:06.935 --> 00:06:09.744
all deposits were not at risk.

00:06:09.744 --> 00:06:11.694
SVB's focus on Silicon Valley

00:06:11.694 --> 00:06:14.760
made it uniquely vulnerable to a run.

00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:16.500
- Its fortunes were really tied to

00:06:16.500 --> 00:06:18.630
the fortunes of this one industry.

00:06:18.630 --> 00:06:20.100
But what's more, this is an industry

00:06:20.100 --> 00:06:22.740
that has flighty deposits.

00:06:22.740 --> 00:06:26.410
You can't really rely on this
short-term funding necessarily

00:06:26.410 --> 00:06:30.240
to support these long-term
investments and bonds.

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:32.790
- [Narrator] Now some,
like President Joe Biden

00:06:32.790 --> 00:06:35.930
are blaming the Dodd Frank
Act rollback for the failures.

00:06:35.930 --> 00:06:38.760
- I'm gonna ask Congress
and the banking regulators

00:06:38.760 --> 00:06:40.470
to strengthen the rules for banks

00:06:40.470 --> 00:06:42.815
to make it less likely
this kind of bank failure

00:06:42.815 --> 00:06:44.520
would happen again.

00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:46.873
- [Narrator] With investigations
into SVB underway,

00:06:46.873 --> 00:06:49.828
the bank's clients will
see their money returned.

00:06:49.828 --> 00:06:52.620
But shareholders are out of luck.

00:06:52.620 --> 00:06:54.060
When asked about the possibility

00:06:54.060 --> 00:06:55.858
of bailing out Silicon Valley Bank,

00:06:55.858 --> 00:06:57.930
the Treasury Secretary said,

00:06:57.930 --> 00:06:59.760
- We're not going to do that again.

00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:03.060
But we are concerned about depositors

00:07:03.060 --> 00:07:06.871
and are focused on trying
to meet their needs.

00:07:06.871 --> 00:07:09.371
(quiet music)

